<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Magnus the Good</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1024–<a href="/years/1047">1047</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Magnus I of Norway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Magnus the Good was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1035 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from 1042 to 1047, the son of Olaf II, Norway's patron saint. Nobles who had turned against Danish rule brought him back as a boy from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. Through an agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a> he inherited Denmark, which he defended against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> at Lyrskov Heath in 1043. He shared Norway with his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> from 1046 and died the following year.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-olafsson">Magnus Olafsson</a> was born around 1024, an illegitimate son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a>, later Saint Olaf, and Alfhild, an English slave of Olaf's queen Astrid Olofsdotter. Born prematurely and weak, he was hastily baptised by the skald Sigvatr Þórðarson, who named him after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> dethroned Olaf in 1028 the family fled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, where Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> sheltered them. In 1031 Magnus's uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Sigurdsson</a> brought news of Olaf's death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stiklestad">Battle of Stiklestad</a>. The boy was educated in Old Russian and some Greek and trained as a warrior.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Cnut had appointed his first wife Ælfgifu and their son Svein as regents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, but the Norwegians found their rule oppressive and drove them out before Cnut died in 1035. Einar Thambarskelfir and Kalf Arnesson went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> to bring the boy back, and Magnus was established as king at the age of eleven. He first sought revenge on his father's enemies, but on Sigvatr's advice stopped, and so became known as the Good.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, wanted the two kingdoms reunited, and Magnus began a campaign against Denmark around 1040, but nobles of both countries brought the kings together at the Göta älv, where they agreed that the first to die would be succeeded by the other. When Harthacnut died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1042 Magnus also became king of Denmark, against the claim of Cnut's nephew Sweyn Estridsson. He destroyed Jomsborg, the headquarters of the Jomsvikings, and in 1043 decisively defeated an invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> at the Battle of Lyrskov Heath, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>, with up to 15,000 killed. He also wrote to Edward, the new English king, that he meant to attack England, but never did so.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Sigurdsson</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from the east and contested Magnus's rule there, allying himself with Sweyn, and in 1046 Magnus made him co-king of Norway. By late 1046 Magnus had driven Sweyn out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. He died suddenly in Denmark on 25 October 1047; accounts vary between an accident, such as falling overboard or from a horse, and disease. He is said to have named Sweyn his heir in Denmark and Harald in Norway, and he was buried with his father in the cathedral at Nidaros, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>.</p><p>His line ended with his death. In 1280 Eric II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, descended through his mother from Magnus's legitimate sister Wulfhild, was crowned king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a> describes Magnus as a man of middle height, light-haired, well spoken and generous.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Magnus the Good” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Norway rejected Danish rule under <b>Magnus the Good</b>, while his sons Harold Harefoot and Harthacnut divided England and Denmark.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-sea-empire">North Sea Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Died in Zealand, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=1038" aria-label="Norway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 155 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Magnus the Good"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 425h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=1038">What was here in 1038? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
